A profile keeps its extensions on a new Mac. Chrome checks protected preferences twice at load: first synchronously
with the legacy HMAC alone (an empty seed for this build, plus the machine's IOPlatformUUID), then, once the Safe
Storage key arrives, with the encrypted hash. The machine id changes when a profile moves to another Mac (Migration
Assistant, a Time Machine restore, a copied data directory), so every HMAC fails there and the first pass removed every
installed extension from extensions.settings, and reset developer mode, pinned tabs and startup pages, before the
encrypted hash, which the migrated "Ardia Safe Storage" item still verifies, was ever read. In the field
(extensions_loaded, 14 days to 2026-11-26) every fresh drop checked was an install's first launch on different
hardware (M4 to M6, M4 Pro to M5 Pro, M1 to M5, M2 to M6, M3 Max to M5 Max), not an app update.

Now, when the super MAC fails too (the whole store was hashed on another machine), the first pass leaves a value whose
HMAC fails but which carries an encrypted hash for the second pass to decide: it is neither reset nor re-hashed there.
A store whose super MAC still holds, such as one with a single edited entry, resets in the first pass as before, so
that value never loads. The second pass checks the encrypted hash with the Safe Storage key; if it
holds, the value stays and the next write stores fresh HMACs for this machine; if not, it falls back to the stored HMAC
(chromium-pref-hash-safe-storage-key-change.patch), which still fails, and the value resets as before. Without an
encrypted hash, or with kEncryptedPrefHashing off (no second pass), the first pass resets as it always did. This
trusts nothing a forged HMAC doesn't already pass: with the empty seed and a readable machine id the HMAC is the weaker
check, and the encrypted hash still decides.

